  "subtitle": "A Tektronix storage-tube CAD terminal with a drawn menu pick sheet on the graphics tablet — the paper-under-pointer command palette as primary interface",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum has several drawing tablets, but almost nothing about the tablet as a *command surface*. Computervision's CADDS3 systems put the operator in front of a Tektronix storage-tube display, then had them pick commands and sketch on a graphics tablet overlaid with a printed menu sheet — the tablet was both a drawing surface and a keyboard substitute. It is the visual grammar that later became the AutoCAD digitizer tablet and, eventually, on-screen toolbars. The interface is odd by modern standards: a phosphor storage tube that held a line drawing without flicker, a tablet with a paper command palette, and a second keyboard terminal alongside for typing commands. It is a case study in how professional CAD — the work that drove half the graphics hardware of the era — felt to the people doing it.",
